---
/** How the image to text tools work. */
import { OCR_LANGUAGES } from '@/lib/ml/read/languages'

interface Props {
  /** The PDF tools: a searchable PDF, or only its text. */
  pdf?: 'searchable' | 'text'
}
const { pdf } = Astro.props
---

<h2>How to use it</h2>
{
  pdf ? (
    <ol>
      <li>Choose the language of the text. Add a second one for a document with two languages.</li>
      <li>Drop a scanned PDF. It stays on your device.</li>
      <li>Each page without its own text is drawn at 200 dpi and read. The progress shows the page.</li>
      <li>{pdf === 'searchable' ? 'Download the searchable PDF, or the text as TXT.' : 'Copy the text or download it as TXT.'}</li>
    </ol>
  ) : (
    <ol>
      <li>Choose the language of the text. Add a second one for a page with two languages.</li>
      <li>Drop, choose or paste one or more images. They stay on your device.</li>
      <li>The text appears next to the image. Correct it if you need to.</li>
      <li>Copy the text, or download it as a TXT file.</li>
    </ol>
  )
}

<h2>How it works</h2>
<p>
  The tool runs Tesseract, an open source text recognition engine (Apache 2.0 licence), compiled to WebAssembly. Its LSTM
  neural network reads each line of text as a sequence of characters. First the page layout is found: columns,
  paragraphs and lines in reading order. Small images are enlarged 2 times, because the engine reads letters of 20 to 40
  pixels best, and light text on a dark background is inverted.
</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Languages</th><th>Data per language</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>{OCR_LANGUAGES.map((l) => l.name).join(', ')}</td><td>1 to 3 MB, downloaded once</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

{
  pdf === 'searchable' && (
    <>
      <h2>The text layer</h2>
      <p>
        Each recognised word is written over its place on the page in invisible text (PDF text rendering mode 3), with the
        font size set to the height of the word and squeezed to its width. The page image is not changed, so the PDF looks the
        same but can be searched, selected and copied. The layer uses a standard font, which holds the Latin alphabet only.
      </p>
    </>
  )
}

<h2>Tips for better text</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Photograph a page straight on, in even light, with the text filling the frame.</li>
  <li>Choose the right language. Accents and letters of other alphabets need their language.</li>
  <li>Crop away pictures and borders around the text.</li>
</ul>
